 "chapter": "4",
 "title": "Antiderivatives",
 "short": "Antiderivatives",
 "lead": "Differentiation can be run backward: given a function, find a function whose derivative it is. This section defines antiderivatives and the indefinite integral, gives the first integration formulas, and uses antiderivatives to solve initial-value problems, such as finding how far a braking car travels.",
 "objectives": [
  "Find the general antiderivative of a given function.",
  "Explain the terms and notation used for an indefinite integral.",
  "State the power rule for integrals.",
  "Use antidifferentiation to solve simple initial-value problems."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li>If $\\kF$ is an <span data-concept=\"antiderivative\">antiderivative</span> of $\\kf,$ then every <span data-concept=\"antiderivative\">antiderivative</span> of $\\kf$ is of the form $\\kF(x)+C$ for some constant $C.$</li><li>Solving the <span data-concept=\"initial-value-problem\">initial-value problem</span></li></ul>$$\\kdydx=\\kf(x),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0}$$<p>requires us first to find the set of <span data-concept=\"antiderivative\">antiderivatives</span> of $\\kf$ and then to look for the particular <span data-concept=\"antiderivative\">antiderivative</span> that also satisfies the <span data-concept=\"initial-value-problem\">initial condition</span>.</p><section id=\"\"><h2></h2>",
