   "cite": "indefinite-integrals",
   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, determine whether the statement is true or false. Either prove it is true or find a counterexample if it is false.</p><p>If $f(x)$ is the antiderivative of $v(x),$ then $f(2x)$ is the antiderivative of $v(2x).$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "choice",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "options": [
     "True",
     "False"
    ],
    "correct": 1,
    "solution": "False. By the chain rule, $\\frac{d}{dx}f(2x)=2f'(2x)=2v(2x),$ not $v(2x).$ For example, $f(x)=x$ is an antiderivative of $v(x)=1,$ but $f(2x)=2x$ has derivative $2,$ while $v(2x)=1.$"
   }
  },
  {
