   "cite": "indefinite-integrals",
   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, determine whether the statement is true or false. Either prove it is true or find a counterexample if it is false.</p><p>If $f(x)$ is the antiderivative of $v(x),$ then ${(f(x))}^{2}$ is the antiderivative of ${(v(x))}^{2}.$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "choice",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "options": [
     "True",
     "False"
    ],
    "correct": 1,
    "solution": "False. By the chain rule, $\\frac{d}{dx}(f(x))^{2}=2f(x)f'(x)=2f(x)v(x),$ which is in general not $(v(x))^{2}.$ For example, $f(x)=x$ is an antiderivative of $v(x)=1,$ but $(f(x))^{2}=x^{2}$ has derivative $2x,$ while $(v(x))^{2}=1.$"
   }
  },
